const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is familial glucocorticoid deficiency?',
    a: 'Familial glucocorticoid deficiency (FGD) is a group of inherited conditions in which the adrenal cortex cannot make enough cortisol in response to ACTH. Children typically present in infancy or early childhood with low blood sugar, poor tolerance of illness, and often deeply pigmented skin. Aldosterone is usually preserved, so a salt-losing crisis is not the typical picture.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is it a melanocortin disease?',
    a: 'The two classic causes sit on the ACTH receptor itself. Type 1 is loss-of-function mutations in MC2R. Type 2 is mutations in MRAP, the accessory protein MC2R needs in order to reach the cell surface and bind ACTH. Together those two genes account for about half of cases. The rest are other genes that keep the adrenal cell alive, not other melanocortin receptors.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the skin darken?',
    a: 'The pituitary senses the missing cortisol and pours out ACTH. ACTH is a melanocortin, and at very high levels it activates MC1R in the skin — the same spillover that bronzes the skin in Addison’s disease. The pigment is a sign of the unbraked signal, not a separate skin disease.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a drug that repairs the receptor?',
    a: 'No. Care is cortisol replacement, managed by a paediatric endocrinologist. Atumelnant, the MC2R antagonist in trials for congenital adrenal hyperplasia, blocks this receptor; it is the opposite tool, and it has no role here. This page is not treatment advice.',
  },
];

