  <p>
    <strong>Familial glucocorticoid deficiency</strong> is what it sounds like: cortisol does
    not come, in a family pattern, and the rest of the adrenal’s salt hormone usually does.
    It is the loss-of-function mirror of the diseases elsewhere on this site. In{' '}
    <a href="/congenital-adrenal-hyperplasia">congenital adrenal hyperplasia</a> the receptor
    works and the signal never stops. In{' '}
    <a href="/cushings-disease">Cushing’s disease</a> the signal is overproduced. Here the
    signal is high because the adrenal cannot hear it.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to lose the same receptor</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> is the ACTH receptor, and it is useless without its
    accessory protein, MRAP. MRAP escorts the receptor to the cell surface and lets it bind{' '}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>. Type 1 FGD is loss-of-function mutations in MC2R itself, first
    tied to a point mutation in the receptor in 1993.<Cite n={1} /> Type 2 is mutations in
    MRAP, identified in 2005 as the missing partner.<Cite n={2} /> Type 2 tends to present
    even earlier. Together the two genes account for roughly half of FGD. The remainder has
    been traced to genes that defend the adrenal cell against oxidative stress and that
    repair its DNA, not to MC1R, MC3R, MC4R or MC5R.<Cite n={4} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of the MC2R mutations do not simply jam the on-switch. Examined one by one, the
    majority leave the receptor stranded inside the cell even when MRAP is present. It is a
    trafficking failure.<Cite n={3} /> That is why “the gene is there” is not the same as
    “the adrenal can answer.” The structural reason MC2R needs the chaperone at all is on
    the receptor page: the fold is not stable, and the binding pocket is not the right shape,
    until MRAP wraps it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the child actually has</h2>
  <p>
    Presentation is in infancy or early childhood: hypoglycaemia, seizures, recurrent
    infection, failure to thrive, and often deep skin pigmentation.<Cite n={4} /> The
    pigment is the melanocortin family showing its unity. No cortisol means no brake on the
    pituitary, ACTH climbs, and the excess activates <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> in
    the skin — the same spillover described on the{' '}
    <a href="/addisons-disease">Addison’s disease</a> page. The diseases are not the same.
    In FGD the gland never learned to hear ACTH, and mineralocorticoid production is
    characteristically preserved, because aldosterone answers mainly to the
    renin–angiotensin system.<Cite n={4} /> In autoimmune Addison’s the whole cortex fails,
    salt hormone included. In salt-wasting CAH the enzyme block hits cortisol and
    aldosterone together, and the intact MC2R drives androgen excess on top.
  </p>

  <h2>What medicine does and does not do</h2>
  <p>
    There is no drug programme to restore MC2R trafficking. The treatment is replacing the
    cortisol the adrenal cannot make, under a specialist, for life. The short cosyntropin
    test — synthetic ACTH, asking MC2R for a cortisol rise — is how adrenal reserve is
    checked, and in FGD the answer is blunted. That test is described with the receptor,
    not as a protocol here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/atumelnant">Atumelnant</a> is an MC2R antagonist in trials for too much ACTH
    signalling. It is the wrong direction for a child whose receptor already does not work.
    Naming it is the point of keeping the agonist and antagonist pages honest: blocking this
    receptor is a therapy only when the receptor is the problem by being too obedient.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    FGD is the cleanest human proof that MC2R and MRAP are one machine. Lose either piece
    and cortisol fails, ACTH rises, and the skin records the surplus. For the receptor, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>; for the hormone, <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>; for the
    map of every other condition, <a href="/conditions">conditions</a>.
  </p>

